    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Basic Trains',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Basic Trains is a domino game in the train family played with a double-nine or double-twelve set. There is no boneyard: players receive their tiles, each starts a personal train with a double if possible, and then extends trains by matching open ends.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Basic Trains belongs to the disconnected-layout train family of domino games. It uses the personal-train idea without the shared public train and without a boneyard, making it a simpler relative of later train games.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Four to ten players. The rules table gives hand sizes for double-nine and double-twelve sets according to player count.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'A double-nine or double-twelve domino set. Leftover tiles are set aside face down and are not used as a boneyard.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Domino set and enough table space for each player to maintain a personal train.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deal all playable hands according to player count; any leftover tiles are put aside face down and are not drawn during play.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Double-nine set: Hand sizes are smaller with the double-nine set and support four to ten players.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Double-twelve set: The larger double-twelve set supports larger hand sizes under the same train rules.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
